  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Consider the quadratic equation of the form $y=ax^2+2bx+c$. Choose values of $a$, $b$ and $c$ by taking any three consecutive terms from the geometric sequence $1,2,4,8,16,\\ldots$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p9-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that no matter which three consecutive terms are chosen, the turning point of the resulting quadratic will always be the same.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The turning point is always $(-2,0)$.\n\nConsecutive terms $k,2k,4k$ give $y=k(x+2)^2$.",
          "worked": "Write the consecutive terms as $k,2k,4k$, where $k$ is the first chosen term. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=kx^2+2(2k)x+4k\\\\y&=k(x^2+4x+4)\\\\y&=k(x+2)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe turning point is $(-2,0)$, independently of $k$, so every choice gives the same turning point.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Now do the same, except take any three consecutive terms from the geometric sequence $3,9,27,81,\\ldots$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The turning point is always $(-3,0)$.\n\nConsecutive terms $k,3k,9k$ give $y=k(x+3)^2$.",
          "worked": "Write the consecutive terms as $k,3k,9k$, where $k$ is the first chosen term. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=kx^2+2(3k)x+9k\\\\y&=k(x^2+6x+9)\\\\y&=k(x+3)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe turning point is $(-3,0)$, independently of $k$, so every choice gives the same turning point.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Can you generalise the above two results?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For consecutive geometric terms $k,mk,m^2k$, with $k\\ne0$, the turning point is $(-m,0)$.\n\nThe common ratio determines the turning point because $y=k(x+m)^2$.",
          "worked": "For a geometric sequence with common ratio $m$, write three consecutive terms as $k,mk,m^2k$, with $k\\ne0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=kx^2+2(mk)x+m^2k\\\\y&=k(x^2+2mx+m^2)\\\\y&=k(x+m)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe turning point is $(-m,0)$. It depends only on the common ratio, not on which three consecutive terms are chosen.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      }
    ]
  },
